or discussing the competency of an

2698
03:01:25,079 --> 03:01:28,203
individual. So whether that's in relation to hiring

2699
03:01:28,235 --> 03:01:32,122
or firing, or if it's in relation to contracting, or whatever the case may

2700
03:01:32,622 --> 03:01:35,760
be. So it requires a 2/3 supermajority vote to close

2701
03:01:36,260 --> 03:01:39,996
a meeting. So in this case, it would typically require

2702
03:01:40,496 --> 03:01:43,945
require 4 votes. Okay. So the question then is,

2703
03:01:43,978 --> 03:01:47,061
what is a meeting? It is actually a term that is defined in the Open

2704
03:01:47,561 --> 03:01:51,661
Meetings Act. It is the convening of a public body with a quorum present.

2705
03:01:52,063 --> 03:01:55,235
So the convening is done by the person with authority to convene a meeting,

2706
03:01:56,089 --> 03:02:00,146
which in our case is either the mayor or 2 council members.

2707
03:02:02,333 --> 03:02:06,584
So with a quorum present, which is 3 individuals on

2708
03:02:07,084 --> 03:02:10,757
the council exclusive of the mayor, including a

2709
03:02:11,257 --> 03:02:14,914
workshop or an executive session, whether in person or by means of electronic communications,

2710
03:02:14,994 --> 03:02:18,830
for the purpose of discussing, receiving comments from the public about,

2711
03:02:18,862 --> 03:02:22,602
or acting upon a matter over which the public body has jurisdiction

2712
03:02:22,714 --> 03:02:26,502
or advisory power. So the simplified version of this is a—

2713
03:02:26,646 --> 03:02:30,386
you have a quorum, so 3 individuals, and you are discussing public business.

2714
03:02:30,434 --> 03:02:33,682
Yes, are you going to say something? Craig? Oh, it looked like you were

2715
03:02:34,182 --> 03:02:37,206
gonna say something, okay. So this, a meeting can include phone calls,

2716
03:02:37,303 --> 03:02:40,552
video conferencing, emails, text messages, et cetera. It doesn't have

2717
03:02:41,052 --> 03:02:44,221
to be a formal setting. So the rule of thumb

2718
03:02:44,721 --> 03:02:47,950
is if you are in a quorum, don't talk about city

2719
03:02:48,450 --> 03:02:50,366
business, which I know can be hard to do because what one thing do you

2720
03:02:50,447 --> 03:02:52,830
all have in common? So,

2721
03:02:54,196 --> 03:02:58,034
it's that you're all elected to represent the city. So as I

2722
03:02:58,534 --> 03:03:01,358
said, a quorum is 3 members of the city council exclusive of the mayor.

2723
03:03:01,422 --> 03:03:05,148
It's specific in the statute. The mayor does not count in determining

2724
03:03:05,244 --> 03:03:09,274
whether a quorum exists. Chances of social meetings are

2725
03:03:09,774 --> 03:03:13,754
not subject to the Open Public Meetings Act, but they cannot be used to circumvent

2726
03:03:14,254 --> 03:03:17,479
the requirements of the act. So if you wanna go bowling together, that's fine.

2727
03:03:17,527 --> 03:03:21,397
Just don't discuss city business. It's not a meeting and therefore

2728
03:03:21,897 --> 03:03:24,990
not subject to the act. Again,

2729
03:03:25,490 --> 03:03:28,720
typically we would only discuss items that are properly noticed.

2730
03:03:29,190 --> 03:03:32,490
There's, again, noticing requirements, there's emergency noticing

2731
03:03:32,990 --> 03:03:36,450
requirements, all sorts of things. But you can't take final action on anything

2732
03:03:36,950 --> 03:03:40,510
that isn't properly noticed. So if there's a discussion item and you—

2733
03:03:41,010 --> 03:03:42,910
there's something that comes up where it says City Manager items and we talk about

2734
03:03:43,410 --> 03:03:46,610
it, you couldn't take final action on that unless you specified what

2735
03:03:47,110 --> 03:03:48,924
item you were going to take final action and notice that that separately.

2736
03:03:51,538 --> 03:03:55,138
Okay. So this is an important one because this

2737
03:03:55,638 --> 03:03:58,737
is, I think, 2 years old now since the legislature added this. I think

2738
03:03:59,237 --> 03:04:02,803
it's an important rule, which is individuals

2739
03:04:02,819 --> 03:04:06,111
constituting a quorum of a public body such as the city council

2740
03:04:06,224 --> 03:04:09,377
may not act together outside a meeting in a concerted and deliberate way to

2741
03:04:09,393 --> 03:04:13,180
predetermine an action to be taken at the meeting on a relevant matter. And relevant

2742
03:04:13,680 --> 03:04:15,899
matter is a defined term. It's just anything within your jurisdiction.

2743
03:04:17,192 --> 03:04:21,271
So, what that means is that if one councilmember

2744
03:04:21,771 --> 03:04:24,852
talks to a second councilmember about a relevant matter, that's fine.

2745
03:04:25,768 --> 03:04:28,578
They can even predetermine their action if they want. I wouldn't advise it, but that

2746
03:04:29,078 --> 03:04:32,175
is not against the law. If they then talk to a third councilmember,

2747
03:04:32,288 --> 03:04:36,110
even if it's not all together at one time, then that,

2748
03:04:36,126 --> 03:04:39,836
and they predetermine the action, that would be considered a violation. And here's a

2749
03:04:40,318 --> 03:04:44,156
good example of it. I think most of you have seen this. But I

2750
03:04:44,656 --> 03:04:48,620
think it's hard to find Open and Public Meetings Act videos,

2751
03:04:48,973 --> 03:04:52,345
but this is a good one, and from a— you wouldn't expect it from casino.

2752
03:04:52,537 --> 03:04:54,801
Chairman, members of the commission,

2753
03:04:55,717 --> 03:04:59,089
Mr. Wollstein is pleased to be here today. And when the day finally came,

2754
03:04:59,313 --> 03:05:03,424
I was ready. I felt so confident that all I had to do was present

2755
03:05:03,924 --> 03:05:07,663
my case. And we have documents, one of which is a report by retired

2756
03:05:08,163 --> 03:05:12,050
FBI agents which completely absolves Mr. Rothstein from any wrongdoing.

2757
03:05:12,550 --> 03:05:14,970
I'd like this marked, Mr. Chairman. Pardon me, Counselor, before you continue. No, I want

2758
03:05:15,470 --> 03:05:18,950
to have this marked. This commission is prepared to act on a motion denying the

2759
03:05:19,450 --> 03:05:22,330
Rothstein application. Deny it! Do I hear a motion seconded?

2760
03:05:22,490 --> 03:05:25,650
Mr. Chairman. Mr. Chairman, I second the motion. Do I have a vote on

2761
03:05:26,150 --> 03:05:29,250
the motion? Mr. Chairman. Aye. Aye. Aye.

2762
03:05:29,750 --> 03:05:31,180
The ayes have it. This hearing is adjourned.

2763
03:05:32,290 --> 03:05:35,890
Okay, so I show that video because it I

2764
03:05:36,390 --> 03:05:39,730
think demonstrates the rationale behind the Open and Public Meetings

2765
03:05:40,230 --> 03:05:44,830
Act. Here, obviously, you see this really, you know, very full table

2766
03:05:45,330 --> 03:05:48,630
full of what appear to be case reporters. I'm not sure why they would need

2767
03:05:49,130 --> 03:05:52,490
half a dozen case reporters filled with mostly irrelevant

2768
03:05:52,990 --> 03:05:56,330
cases, but apparently they have that. They have a bunch of documents. They spent hours

2769
03:05:56,830 --> 03:05:59,950
preparing for this. And before they've had any discussion on the matter, they vote and

2770
03:06:00,450 --> 03:06:03,850
decide it. Obviously, this is not the purpose of the Open and Public Meetings Act.

2771
03:06:04,623 --> 03:06:08,297
What we want to do is have that deliberation, let them present

2772
03:06:08,797 --> 03:06:12,950
their case, deliberate about it openly, and then make a decision, not have it predetermined

2773
03:06:13,450 --> 03:06:15,340
like it was, as what happened in this case.

2774
03:06:16,351 --> 03:06:19,672
So now I'm going to go with some hypotheticals.

2775
03:06:20,667 --> 03:06:24,453
Hypothetical 1, and I've changed these from previous presentations.

2776
03:06:24,517 --> 03:06:28,320
So Councilmember A meets with Councilmember B to discuss

2777
03:06:28,432 --> 03:06:31,418
a preliminary plat application coming coming before the city council on the next agenda.

2778
03:06:31,884 --> 03:06:35,146
Councilmember A convinces Councilmember B that preliminary plat

2779
03:06:35,210 --> 03:06:38,568
does not meet the legal requirements. They decide to vote against it at the next

2780
03:06:39,068 --> 03:06:40,991
meeting. Is this a violation of the Open and Public Meetings Act?

2781
03:06:45,990 --> 03:06:47,448
It's okay to answer if you know the answer.

2782
03:06:50,309 --> 03:06:51,891
Not a violation. Why?

2783
03:06:52,690 --> 03:06:56,393
It's just 2 of them. Does not constitute a

2784
03:06:56,893 --> 03:06:59,691
quorum. But it still does create— But what you don't want to do is predetermine

2785
03:07:00,191 --> 03:07:03,732
an outcome. Yes. That's the key. That's where you're trying to trick

2786
03:07:04,232 --> 03:07:07,709
us. So I would say that this is not a violation, but does somewhat go

2787
03:07:07,725 --> 03:07:10,538
against the spirit. But it kind of borders on one that is potential.

2788
03:07:12,528 --> 03:07:15,384
Well, this is not a violation because there's not a quorum. Now, I would not

2789
03:07:15,884 --> 03:07:19,945
recommend that you predetermine actions. It's fine to

2790
03:07:20,173 --> 03:07:22,810
form opinions, of Of course. You look at the preliminary plat, you think it doesn't

2791
03:07:23,310 --> 03:07:26,790
meet the legal requirements, it's natural to form that opinion based upon review.

2792
03:07:27,290 --> 03:07:30,850
I would expect you to form an opinion. Certainly, maybe not set in stone,

2793
03:07:31,350 --> 03:07:34,550
but certainly form an opinion prior to the meeting, because that means you studied

2794
03:07:35,050 --> 03:07:38,850
the issue. What, you know, even predetermining in this sense is not

2795
03:07:39,350 --> 03:07:42,490
actually a violation, but maybe against the spirit of what you're being asked to do.

2796
03:07:43,810 --> 03:07:47,570
Okay. Hypothetical number 2. Councilmember A speaks to the mayor about a development

2797
03:07:48,070 --> 03:07:51,171
agreement on the next On next week's city council agenda, and they agree on several

2798
03:07:51,671 --> 03:07:55,048
specific changes that need, sorry, there's a typo, need to be made to the agreement.

2799
03:07:55,912 --> 03:07:58,373
The mayor contacts Councilmember B and discusses the matter.

2800
03:07:58,888 --> 03:08:02,170
Councilmember B agrees that the changes are important and indicates that

2801
03:08:02,670 --> 03:08:05,001
he will support them. Is this a violation of the Open and Public Meetings Act?

2802
03:08:05,597 --> 03:08:09,158
Trick question. It's not very tricky.

2803
03:08:10,995 --> 03:08:12,328
So it is not a violation.

2804
03:08:14,202 --> 03:08:17,445
Again, because the mayor does not count towards the quorum, that's the principle

2805
03:08:17,945 --> 03:08:20,881
being illustrated. Again, when you're going through agreements,

2806
03:08:21,668 --> 03:08:25,200
if you think something needs to be changed, obviously you're the

2807
03:08:25,700 --> 03:08:29,663
policymakers, you make those decisions, and it's okay to

2808
03:08:29,679 --> 03:08:33,629
form an opinion on that. Obviously you should be open to reconsideration or

2809
03:08:34,129 --> 03:08:37,804
amendment or modification of those, what changes you think need to be made, or be

2810
03:08:38,304 --> 03:08:41,400
willing to be convinced that they don't need to be made. and discuss that in

2811
03:08:41,900 --> 03:08:45,400
the meeting. But forming these opinions and even discussing it among 2

2812
03:08:45,416 --> 03:08:48,387
council members and then the mayor is not a violation of the Act.

2813
03:08:48,516 --> 03:08:52,097
So there you go. Hypothetical 3.

2814
03:08:52,981 --> 03:08:55,776
Council Member A speaks to Council Member B about a rezone application in front of

2815
03:08:56,276 --> 03:08:59,984
the City Council next week. He convinces Council Member B to recommend approval.

2816
03:09:00,048 --> 03:09:03,581
Council Member B speaks to Council Member C to get her support. Council Member

2817
03:09:04,081 --> 03:09:06,135
C dislikes the rezone application and declines to give support.

2818
03:09:07,002 --> 03:09:09,024
Is this a violation of the Open Public Meetings Act?

2819
03:09:11,177 --> 03:09:14,700
Yes, because they combined A, B, and C

2820
03:09:15,395 --> 03:09:17,625
and had a discussion by proxy.

2821
03:09:19,240 --> 03:09:22,299
That's correct. So the fact that Councilmember— Oh, sorry.

2822
03:09:22,959 --> 03:09:27,017
The fact that Councilmember C predetermined

2823
03:09:27,517 --> 03:09:29,990
something different than Councilmembers A and B does not matter. Okay.

2824
03:09:31,820 --> 03:09:32,745
So it's still a violation.

2825
03:09:35,120 --> 03:09:38,939
Okay, hypothetical 4, same facts except that the discussion occurs via text

2826
03:09:39,439 --> 03:09:40,679
messaging. Is this a violation?

2827
03:09:44,796 --> 03:09:48,210
Still yes. Still yes. Okay. Text messaging doesn't change

2828
03:09:48,564 --> 03:09:51,881
the legal landscape. It's still a violation. Okay,

2829
03:09:51,961 --> 03:09:55,221
hypothetical 5, during an open meeting, council votes to go into closed session.

2830
03:09:55,286 --> 03:09:58,738
During the closed session, the city council discusses potentially suing a contractor over a bridge

2831
03:09:58,770 --> 03:10:02,127
contract issue. Councilmember A asks how this will affect other agreements with the same

2832
03:10:02,627 --> 03:10:04,919
contractor. Is this a violation of the Open Public Meetings Act?

2833
03:10:17,097 --> 03:10:20,455
Got it. Well, they, they can go to a closed session because they're

2834
03:10:20,955 --> 03:10:24,879
talking about litigation, so that's acceptable. They can discuss

2835
03:10:24,927 --> 03:10:28,733
that, and I think they can discuss the ramifications of it, is my own

2836
03:10:28,765 --> 03:10:32,009
opinion of it, but I don't know on that second part of the question.

2837
03:10:32,154 --> 03:10:35,751
The problem comes for me when Member A asks how this will affect other agreements

2838
03:10:36,251 --> 03:10:40,794
with the same contractor, because those are not part of the closed session item.

2839
03:10:41,404 --> 03:10:44,922
So my favorite answer in the law is, of course, it depends, right?

2840
03:10:45,259 --> 03:10:48,310
So I think a narrow discussion of how potentially

2841
03:10:48,810 --> 03:10:52,116
suing a contractor, which again would

2842
03:10:52,616 --> 03:10:54,968
fall within the reasonably imminent litigation, because you're actively discussing, should we sue this person?

2843
03:10:58,330 --> 03:11:01,568
You know, whether, how that will affect the total landscape with that same

2844
03:11:01,665 --> 03:11:05,500
person, and the effects of actually instituting a lawsuit,

2845
03:11:05,532 --> 03:11:09,156
I think if you keep it in that narrow scope, you're fine. The issue

2846
03:11:09,656 --> 03:11:12,490
comes when you don't keep it in the narrow scope, and then you run into

2847
03:11:12,538 --> 03:11:15,807
problems. So, it's one where you have to be very vigilant in

2848
03:11:15,839 --> 03:11:19,626
making sure that your discussion is specific to the lawsuit,

2849
03:11:19,931 --> 03:11:23,481
and so your discussion of those other agreements is specific to the lawsuit's

2850
03:11:23,981 --> 03:11:27,369
impact on them. And that's why you sit in every closed session and keep us

2851
03:11:27,562 --> 03:11:30,438
on track if we start to wander slightly. I mean,

2852
03:11:30,856 --> 03:11:34,551
Marcus has us on a short leash. Anybody who sees me out

2853
03:11:35,051 --> 03:11:37,122
here knows I like to enforce rules, or at least I'm willing to.

2854
03:11:38,005 --> 03:11:42,022
Okay, hypothetical 6. Same facts as hypothetical 5.

2855
03:11:42,102 --> 03:11:44,997
Councilmember Beas About whether the city should terminate the contract,

2856
03:11:46,447 --> 03:11:49,724
and what contractor the city should procure as a replacement.

2857
03:11:50,303 --> 03:11:51,797
Is this a violation? Yes.

2858
03:11:53,516 --> 03:11:57,498
Yes, and again, this is what I talked about last one, right? We gotta keep

2859
03:11:57,998 --> 03:12:01,771
ourselves very narrowly tailored on that discussion of those other contracts,

2860
03:12:02,812 --> 03:12:05,675
and discussion of the lawsuit. And the second we start talking about, well, who should

2861
03:12:06,175 --> 03:12:08,408
we get to replace them, then you're all of a sudden, you've crossed the line.

2862
03:12:08,682 --> 03:12:12,877
So don't do that. Okay, hypothetical 7. During closed

2863
03:12:13,377 --> 03:12:16,668
session, the city council discusses the professional competence of Company 1, whose services the

2864
03:12:17,168 --> 03:12:20,045
city is considering using. Is this a violation of the Open and Public Meetings Act?

2865
03:12:26,952 --> 03:12:30,543
Yes. Why? Because it's an individual, not a company.

2866
03:12:30,720 --> 03:12:34,022
That's correct. So the— you're

2867
03:12:34,522 --> 03:12:38,013
allowed to discuss the professional competence

2868
03:12:38,513 --> 03:12:39,874
Competence of an individual. Individual is defined as a human being in this context.

2869
03:12:39,922 --> 03:12:43,441
Sometimes we use the word individual in the law to not just mean human beings,

2870
03:12:43,473 --> 03:12:47,216
but also legal entities. But in the context of the Open and Public Meetings Act,

2871
03:12:47,232 --> 03:12:51,233
it literally means a human being. So talking about a company, unless you're talking about—

2872
03:12:51,828 --> 03:12:55,459
unless company 1 is like an individual, say like

2873
03:12:55,959 --> 03:13:00,022
it's a lobbyist, and they're the company, it's just one individual, then maybe you

2874
03:13:00,522 --> 03:13:04,263
could talk about that individual's competence. But as

2875
03:13:04,279 --> 03:13:07,993
far as, you know, you're talking about like Meta, obviously no.

2876
03:13:08,252 --> 03:13:12,111
You're not talking about anyone's, any specific individual's

2877
03:13:12,611 --> 03:13:16,999
confidence. You're talking about the company. So that would be a violation. Okay. So thus

2878
03:13:17,047 --> 03:13:20,841
concludes the Open Meetings Act portion of the

2879
03:13:21,341 --> 03:13:21,939
training. Are there any questions?

2880
03:13:25,102 --> 03:13:27,518
No, but it's always fun to have you try and trick us, Marcus.

2881
03:13:28,197 --> 03:13:32,014
I do what I can. Try to

2882
03:13:32,514 --> 03:13:36,328
make it interesting, so try to make you think so it's not just rote memorization

2883
03:13:36,828 --> 03:13:40,831
but actually critical thinking. So, alrighty, let's go to Municipal

2884
03:13:41,331 --> 03:13:45,038
Officers and Employees Ethics Act. All right, from the

2885
03:13:45,054 --> 03:13:47,995
beginning. Okay.

2886
03:13:49,081 --> 03:13:52,679
So the purpose of this, of the

2887
03:13:52,760 --> 03:13:56,406
act, is to establish standards for conduct for municipal officers and employees and

2888
03:13:56,906 --> 03:14:00,779
to require these persons disclose actual or potential conflicts of interest between their

2889
03:14:01,279 --> 03:14:03,827
public duties and their personal interests. Okay.

2890
03:14:05,286 --> 03:14:08,942
So here's the first thing. It's unlawful to disclose improperly used private,

2891
03:14:08,958 --> 03:14:12,775
controlled, or protected information acquired by reason of your official

2892
03:14:12,807 --> 03:14:16,336
position in the course of your duties in

2893
03:14:16,836 --> 03:14:20,794
order to further substantially your personal economic interests or to secure special privileges

2894
03:14:21,387 --> 03:14:25,109
or exemption for you or for others. Okay. So this

2895
03:14:25,174 --> 03:14:28,528
is pretty straightforward. In the event that you come into contact,

2896
03:14:28,993 --> 03:14:31,866
and I don't think most of you would on any sort of regular basis,

2897
03:14:31,882 --> 03:14:35,910
but in the event you come into contact with private control or protected records

2898
03:14:36,410 --> 03:14:40,002
or other information that would fall under that, you can't use that for your

2899
03:14:40,502 --> 03:14:44,191
own gain. I think this falls in the common sense category. Whenever you

2900
03:14:45,122 --> 03:14:49,150
obtain something via your public service and you

2901
03:14:49,650 --> 03:14:51,654
try using it for personal gain. I think common sense tells you that might be

2902
03:14:52,154 --> 03:14:54,689
wrong. Okay.

2903
03:14:55,395 --> 03:14:59,072
It is unlawful to— and so anytime— so I just want to clarify something.

2904
03:14:59,169 --> 03:15:01,738
Anytime in the law we say unlawful, it means it's a crime.

2905
03:15:02,492 --> 03:15:05,816
So sometimes you say, you know, you can violate the law and it's just there's

2906
03:15:06,715 --> 03:15:10,312
implications where you could be liable in a civil suit. But anytime

2907
03:15:10,344 --> 03:15:13,058
you use the word in a statute or in an ordinance that says it's unlawful,

2908
03:15:13,074 --> 03:15:15,434
it means you're committing a crime and could be prosecuted.

2909
03:15:15,934 --> 03:15:19,000
Okay, it is unlawful to knowingly receive,

2910
03:15:19,016 --> 03:15:22,052
accept, take, seek, or solicit directly or indirectly for the

2911
03:15:22,552 --> 03:15:26,388
officer or employer or for another a gift of substantial value

2912
03:15:26,888 --> 03:15:31,111
or a substantial economic benefit tantamount to a gift that would tender improperly

2913
03:15:31,143 --> 03:15:34,676
influence a reasonable person in your position to depart from faithful

2914
03:15:35,176 --> 03:15:38,547
and impartial discharge of your duties, that you know or should know

2915
03:15:39,158 --> 03:15:43,095
